{"kind":"report","text":"ready-for-review at exact clean head a36ec7fa78abbb1510217b665c8c9eb7f5bac3d7, artifact 5f22c05df4c908224d45f4ee2d72c49c56651986. Codex's blocking finding is fixed, and I confirmed it in the code before changing anything: read.go's parseClause built the Clause from state and labels first and only then looped over issues, so a body carrying connector=github with nothing else, or with issues that all failed strconv, fell through to an empty criteria clause. Query renders that as state=all and Admits returns true for every issue, so Fetch listed the whole tracker and admitted all of it. The description was exact.\n\nparseClause now reports whether the body bounds anything and ClausesFrom drops it when it does not. A clause must name something: at least one valid issue number, or at least one label, or a state it states. Two narrower edges came with it, both from asking what else could widen on the way through. An issues field that was meant to name issues and named none is now a broken selection rather than a fall-through, because widening past a malformed field is how one typo becomes the whole tracker — so `issues=abc` beside `state=open` is refused rather than rescued into a criteria clause. And a state this connector does not recognize is refused here instead of being handed to GitHub to interpret.\n\nI also ran it against the live workroom rather than only against tests, which is the condition Hugh's charter request asked for. Under his scoped charter b86860f2 the connector passes the doorstep, reads only the issue its clause names, and reports nothing new because that issue is already observed. The empty charter f696c18a is refused with \"names no connector, repository or actor, so it authorizes nothing in particular\"; a mismatched owner with \"charter authorizes owner \\\"generalbusiness-ai\\\", not \\\"someone-else\\\"\"; and a mismatched actor likewise. So the doorstep is now demonstrated on the live log, not only in unit tests.\n\nThe new test asserts each unbounded shape separately rather than through a table, and pins the bounded shapes too, so the fix cannot close the doorstep entirely instead of bounding it.\n\n`go test ./internal/docset ./internal/connector/... ./cmd/gitseq-github/ -count=1` passes and `go vet ./...` is clean at this head. The worktree is clean.","body":{"head":"a36ec7fa78abbb1510217b665c8c9eb7f5bac3d7","status":"ready-for-review"}}